    rdfs:seeAlso <https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/assessment-list-trustworthy-artificial-intelligence-altai-self-assessment> ;
    dcterms:description "For applications that can adversely affect individuals, have redress by design mechanisms been put in place?"@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:HumanOversightMechanism , aidoc:affects , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc-lex:RedressMechanism ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Appeal and redress procedure"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Human review workflow for contested outcomes"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Corrective action and remedy log"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "User notice explaining available remedies"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:altai-r7-08-cq1 , lexreq:altai-r7-08-cq2 .
lexreq:altai-r7-08-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system embed redress-by-design mechanisms for applications that can adversely affect individuals?"@en .
lexreq:altai-r7-08-cq2 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What remedies, appeals, corrections, or human review options does the AI system provide to affected persons?"@en ; lexreq:formalisedBy "cq/altai_r7_redress.rq" .

lexreq:oecd-p1 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:OECD ;
    lexreq:sortKey "3-01" ;
    rdfs:label "Principle 1.1: Inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being"@en ;
    lexreq:section "Principle 1.1" ;
    dcterms:source "OECD AI Principles, Principle 1.1: Inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles> ;
    dcterms:description "Stakeholders should proactively engage in responsible stewardship of trustworthy AI in pursuit of beneficial outcomes for people and the planet, such as augmenting human capabilities and enhancing creativity, advancing inclusion of underrepresented populations, reducing economic, social, gender and other inequalities, and protecting natural environments, thus invigorating inclusive growth, well-being, sustainable development and environmental sustainability."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AISystem , aidoc:affects , aidoc:hasCapability , aidoc:hasIntendedPurpose , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc-lex:ImpactAssessment ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Intended purpose statement"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Benefit case"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Stakeholder map"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Inclusion and accessibility assessment"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Inequality impact assessment"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Environmental or sustainability impact note"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Approval record on benefit-risk trade-offs"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:oecd-p1-cq1 , lexreq:oecd-p1-cq2 , lexreq:oecd-p1-cq3 .
lexreq:oecd-p1-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What documented beneficial outcomes for people and/or the planet does the AI system pursue within its intended purpose?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p1-cq2 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system identify affected groups, including underrepresented populations, and check whether it reduces rather than worsens inequalities?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p1-cq3 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system assess and monitor environmental sustainability, inclusion, and broader well-being impacts across its lifecycle?"@en .

lexreq:oecd-p2 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:OECD ;
    lexreq:sortKey "3-02" ;
    rdfs:label "Principle 1.2: Human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy"@en ;
    lexreq:section "Principle 1.2" ;
    dcterms:source "OECD AI Principles, Principle 1.2: Human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles> ;
    dcterms:description "AI actors should respect the rule of law, human rights, democratic and human-centred values throughout the AI system lifecycle. These include non-discrimination and equality, freedom, dignity, autonomy of individuals, privacy and data protection, diversity, fairness, social justice, and internationally recognised labour rights. This also includes addressing misinformation and disinformation amplified by AI, while respecting freedom of expression and other rights and freedoms protected by applicable international law.\nTo this end, AI actors should implement mechanisms and safeguards, such as capacity for human agency and oversight, including to address risks arising from uses outside of intended purpose, intentional misuse, or unintentional misuse in a manner appropriate to the context and consistent with the state of the art."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AISystem , aidoc:Dataset , aidoc:HumanOversightMechanism , aidoc:Interface , aidoc:TrainingDataSheet , aidoc:hasHumanOversight , aidoc:hasRisk , aidoc:hasSourceDataset , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc-lex:FundamentalRightsImpactAssessment , aidoc-lex:BiasAssessment , aidoc-lex:DataProtectionImpactAssessment , aidoc-lex:InformationIntegrityRisk , aidoc-lex:RiskRegister , aidoc-lex:OverrideMechanism , aidoc:IntendedPurpose ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Fundamental rights impact assessment"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Privacy or DPIA-style assessment"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Fairness and bias evaluation report"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Mitigation plan"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Human oversight policy"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:oecd-p2-cq1 , lexreq:oecd-p2-cq2 , lexreq:oecd-p2-cq3 , lexreq:oecd-p2-cq4 , lexreq:oecd-p2-cq5 .
lexreq:oecd-p2-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system prevent, detect, and mitigate discrimination, harmful bias, loss of autonomy, privacy infringement, and unfair treatments?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p2-cq2 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system identify and control risks of misinformation or disinformation amplification while respecting freedom of expression and other protected human rights?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p2-cq3 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system implement human agency and oversight in operation, review, escalation, and override decisions?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p2-cq4 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What out-of-intended-purpose, intentional-misuse, and unintentional-misuse scenarios does the AI system check for, and how are they controlled?"@en ; lexreq:formalisedBy "cq/oecd_p2_misuse.rq" .
lexreq:oecd-p2-cq5 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How can a human operator intervene, override, halt, or escalate the AI system when undesired behaviour or elevated risk is detected?"@en .

lexreq:oecd-p3 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:OECD ;
    lexreq:sortKey "3-03" ;
    rdfs:label "Principle 1.3: Transparency and explainability"@en ;
    lexreq:section "Principle 1.3" ;
    dcterms:source "OECD AI Principles, Principle 1.3: Transparency and explainability" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles> ;
    dcterms:description "AI Actors should commit to transparency and responsible disclosure regarding AI systems. To this end, they should provide meaningful information, appropriate to the context, and consistent with the state of art: i. to foster a general understanding of AI systems, including their capabilities and limitations, ii. to make stakeholders aware of their interactions with AI systems, including in the workplace, iii. where feasible and useful, to provide plain and easy-to-understand information on the sources of data/input, factors, processes and/or logic that led to the prediction, content, recommendation or decision, to enable those affected by an AI system to understand the output, and, iv. to provide information that enable those adversely affected by an AI system to challenge its output."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:ExplainableAIFeature , aidoc:Interface , aidoc:TransparencyMeasure , aidoc:hasIntendedPurpose , aidoc:hasInterface , aidoc:hasTransparencyMeasure ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "AI notice"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "User-facing limitation statement"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:oecd-p3-cq1 .
lexreq:oecd-p3-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What understandable information does the AI system provide about its capabilities, limitations, data or input sources, and the factors, processes, or logic relevant to its outputs?"@en .

lexreq:oecd-p4 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:OECD ;
    lexreq:sortKey "3-04" ;
    rdfs:label "Principle 1.4: Robustness, security and safety"@en ;
    lexreq:section "Principle 1.4" ;
    dcterms:source "OECD AI Principles, Principle 1.4: Robustness, security and safety" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles> ;
    dcterms:description "AI systems should be robust, secure and safe throughout their entire lifecycle so that, in conditions of normal use, foreseeable use or misuse, or other adverse conditions, they function appropriately and do not pose unreasonable safety and/or security risks.\nMechanisms should be in place, as appropriate, to ensure that if AI systems risk causing undue harm or exhibit undesired behaviour, they can be overridden, repaired, and/or decommissioned safely as needed.\nMechanisms should also, where technically feasible, be in place to bolster information integrity while ensuring respect for freedom of expression."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:DataMonitoringAndLogging , aidoc:HumanOversightMechanism , aidoc:Log , aidoc:ModelEvaluation , aidoc:PerformanceMetric , aidoc:PostMarketPerformanceEvaluationActivity , aidoc:TransparencyMeasure , aidoc:hasPerformanceMetric , aidoc:hasRisk , aidoc:logsActivity , aidoc-lex:SafetyCase , aidoc-lex:SecurityAssessment , aidoc-lex:Procedure , dpv:Policy , aidoc-lex:SyntheticContentLabel , vair:SecurityMeasure ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Validation report"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Security assessment"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Risk register"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Monitoring plan"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Incident response policy"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Shutdown or rollback procedure"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Decommissioning standard operating procedure"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Repair and change tickets"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "False-content escalation workflow"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Integrity monitoring logs"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:oecd-p4-cq1 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq2 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq3 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq4 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq5 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq6 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq7 , lexreq:oecd-p4-cq8 .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system demonstrate robustness, safety, and security under normal use, foreseeable misuse, and adverse operating conditions?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq2 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What performance, safety, and security thresholds does the AI system monitor, and how are failures, vulnerabilities, and residual risks documented?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq3 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system monitor robustness, safety, and security after deployment throughout the lifecycle?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq4 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How can the AI system be overridden, repaired, rolled back, and/or decommissioned safely when undue harm or undesired behaviour is detected?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq5 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What triggers, thresholds, or incident conditions require override, repair, rollback, or decommissioning of the AI system?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq6 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system implement technically feasible measures to support information integrity?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq7 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What controls does the AI system use to identify, label, trace, or limit deceptive, manipulated, or provenance-sensitive outputs where relevant?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p4-cq8 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system ensure that information-integrity controls respect freedom of expression and other applicable rights?"@en .

lexreq:oecd-p5 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:OECD ;
    lexreq:sortKey "3-05" ;
    rdfs:label "Principle 1.5: Accountability"@en ;
    lexreq:section "Principle 1.5" ;
    dcterms:source "OECD AI Principles, Principle 1.5: Accountability" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles> ;
    dcterms:description "AI actors should be accountable for the proper functioning of AI systems and for the respect of the above principles, based on their roles, the context, and consistent with the state of the art.\nTo this end, AI actors should ensure traceability, including in relation to datasets, processes and decisions made during the AI system lifecycle, to enable analysis of the AI system’s outputs and responses to inquiry, appropriate to the context and consistent with the state of the art.\nAI actors, should, based on their roles, the context, and their ability to act, apply a systematic risk management approach to each phase of the AI system lifecycle on an ongoing basis and adopt responsible business conduct to address risks related to AI systems, including, as appropriate, via co-operation between different AI actors, suppliers of AI knowledge and AI resources, AI system users, and other stakeholders. Risks include those related to harmful bias, human rights including safety, security, and privacy, as well as labour and intellectual property rights."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AIActivity , aidoc:AIDeployer , aidoc:AIProvider , aidoc:AIResearcher , aidoc:Auditor , aidoc:ChangeLog , aidoc:ChangeRecord , aidoc:Dataset , aidoc:Log , aidoc:ModelVersioning , aidoc:PostMarketMonitoringActivity , aidoc:SoftwareVersioning , aidoc:appliesStandard , aidoc:hasAIActivity , aidoc:hasResponsibleDeveloper , aidoc:hasRisk , aidoc:hasSourceDataset , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc:isDeployedBy , aidoc:isProvidedBy , aidoc:logsActivity , aidoc:recordsChange , aidoc:version , aidoc-lex:accountableAgent , aidoc-lex:RiskRegister , dpv:Policy , aidoc-lex:ComplianceDocument ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Role descriptions"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Sign-off workflow"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Issue escalation procedure"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Audit plan"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Dataset lineage record"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Model or version history"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Audit log"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Retention schedule"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Incident investigation file"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Lifecycle risk register"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Monitoring dashboard"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Incident register"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:oecd-p5-cq1 , lexreq:oecd-p5-cq2 , lexreq:oecd-p5-cq3 , lexreq:oecd-p5-cq4 , lexreq:oecd-p5-cq5 .
lexreq:oecd-p5-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What actor is accountable for the proper functioning of the AI system at each lifecycle stage?"@en ; lexreq:formalisedBy "cq/oecd_p5_accountable_actors.rq" .
lexreq:oecd-p5-cq2 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system maintain traceability for datasets, model versions, processes, and decisions across the lifecycle?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p5-cq3 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How are logs, provenance records, and version histories retained, secured, and made reviewable?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p5-cq4 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system apply systematic risk management in each phase of its lifecycle on an ongoing basis?"@en .
lexreq:oecd-p5-cq5 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How does the AI system monitor, escalate, and remediate risks related to harmful bias, human rights, safety, security, privacy, labour rights, and intellectual-property rights?"@en .

lexreq:gender-01 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:GenderD32 ;
    lexreq:sortKey "4-01" ;
    rdfs:label "D3.2 Appendix A - Phase I, Section 4 “Gender Inclusivity”"@en ;
    lexreq:section "D3.2 Appendix A" ;
    dcterms:source "D3.2 Appendix A - Phase I, Section 4 “Gender Inclusivity”" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://certain-project.eu/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/CERTAIN_WP3_D3.2_Ethical-considerations-of-AI-compliance_V1.0-delivered.pdf> ;
    dcterms:description "1. To what extent has a gender audit been conducted on datasets, algorithms, and workflows?\n2. How effectively are gender stereotypes identified and mitigated in outputs, interfaces, or communications?\n3. To what degree are underrepresented genders meaningfully included in design and testing processes?\n4. To what extent are performance metrics disaggregated by gender and intersecting identities?\n5. To what degree is gender-sensitive methodology integrated into research and design?\n6. To what extent do institutional mechanisms ensure structural accountability for gender inclusion and equity in governance?\n7. To what degree is inclusive and gender-sensitive language ensured in documentation and interfaces?\n8. To what extent are gender-differentiated impacts identified and addressed, including post-project consequences?\n9. To what degree are intersections or conflicts between gender inclusivity and other fairness or privacy goals managed and justified?\n10. To what extent is an inclusive and equitable team culture promoted, tracked, and documented?"@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "EU Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI / ALTAI - concretely shape the requirements on fairness, diversity, accountability, accessibility, and continuous monitoring reflected in this section."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "OECD AI Principles; UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI - reinforce inclusion, human rights, accessibility, and stakeholder awareness."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "SGIA / gender audit approach - grounds intersectional analysis, gender-differentiated impact review, and post-project impact consideration."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AIModel , aidoc:AISystem , aidoc:Dataset , aidoc:Interface , aidoc:ModelEvaluation , aidoc:PerformanceMetric , aidoc:affects , aidoc:hasInterface , aidoc:hasPerformanceMetric , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc-lex:GenderAudit , aidoc-lex:GenderImpactAssessment , aidoc-lex:AffectedGroup , aidoc-lex:Procedure , aidoc-lex:justification ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Completed checklist answers"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Dataset, model, and workflow audit files"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Communication and interface review"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Subgroup participation records"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Disaggregated KPI tables"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Methodology note"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Governance accountability matrix"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Inclusive language style guide"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Impact log"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Conflict-resolution note"@en ;
    lexreq:expectedEvidence "Team-culture policy and evidence"@en ;
    aiact:hasCompetencyQuestion lexreq:gender-01-cq1 , lexreq:gender-01-cq2 , lexreq:gender-01-cq3 , lexreq:gender-01-cq4 , lexreq:gender-01-cq5 , lexreq:gender-01-cq6 , lexreq:gender-01-cq7 , lexreq:gender-01-cq8 , lexreq:gender-01-cq9 , lexreq:gender-01-cq10 .
lexreq:gender-01-cq1 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How has a gender audit been conducted on datasets, algorithms, and workflows?"@en ; lexreq:formalisedBy "cq/gender_audit.rq" .
lexreq:gender-01-cq2 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How are gender stereotypes identified and mitigated in outputs, interfaces, or communications?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq3 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How are underrepresented genders included meaningfully in design and testing?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq4 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How are performance metrics disaggregated by gender and intersecting identities?"@en ; lexreq:formalisedBy "cq/gender_disaggregated_metrics.rq" .
lexreq:gender-01-cq5 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How is gender-sensitive methodology integrated into research and design?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq6 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What institutional mechanisms ensure structural accountability for gender inclusion and equity?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq7 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How is inclusive and gender-sensitive language ensured in documentation and interfaces?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq8 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What gender-differentiated impacts have been identified and addressed, including post-project consequences?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq9 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How are intersections or conflicts between gender inclusivity and other fairness or privacy goals managed and justified?"@en .
lexreq:gender-01-cq10 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "How is an inclusive and equitable team culture promoted, tracked, and documented?"@en .

lexreq:gender-02 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:GenderD32 ;
    lexreq:sortKey "4-02" ;
    rdfs:label "D3.2 §5.2 - QETAM Analytical Framework"@en ;
    lexreq:section "D3.2 §5.2" ;
    dcterms:source "D3.2 §5.2 - QETAM Analytical Framework" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://certain-project.eu/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/CERTAIN_WP3_D3.2_Ethical-considerations-of-AI-compliance_V1.0-delivered.pdf> ;
    dcterms:description "Building on the above insights, the CERTAIN project employs a blended analytical framework that combines the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), enhanced by a gender-sensitive dimension. Key elements from the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), including Perceived Usefulness (PU) and Perceived Ease of Use (PEOU), are combined with elements from the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), such as Social Influence (SI) and Facilitating Conditions (FC). In addition, our model extends these theories by examining Gender Influence (GI) as a moderating factor that influences the process of technology adoption. This integrated perspective allows for a more holistic understanding of how individuals engage with new technologies, particularly concerning gender dynamics.\nThe research is based on the creation and distribution of a specially designed questionnaire, containing questions related to both demographic information and knowledge and perceptions associated with the six parameters mentioned above. Responses are provided on a scale from 1 to 10. After collecting the responses, a correlation matrix is generated to produce the final BIU number regarding the acceptance of the respective technology.\nAt the same time, using the same model, we measure (potential) gender differences among the participants concerning PU, PEOU, PT, SI, and FC, and proceed with a corresponding analysis of the research findings."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "SGIA / gender-sensitive methodology - is the primary methodological basis for using Gender Influence and for interpreting gender-related dynamics in the technology-acceptance study."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "OECD AI Principles; UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI - shape inclusive participation, non-binary-aware analysis, and human-centred considerations around use and adoption."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "EU Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI / ALTAI - are relevant where the results inform transparency, trust, human agency, and post-deployment monitoring."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AIActivity , aidoc:AISystem , aidoc:PerformanceMetric , aidoc:PostMarketPerformanceEvaluationActivity , aidoc:hasIntendedPurpose , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc:version , aidoc-lex:TechnologyAcceptanceStudy ;
