lexreq:gender-03 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:GenderD32 ;
    lexreq:sortKey "4-03" ;
    rdfs:label "D3.2 §5.2 - GIA / SGIA Five-Step Method"@en ;
    lexreq:section "D3.2 §5.2" ;
    dcterms:source "D3.2 §5.2 - GIA / SGIA Five-Step Method" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://certain-project.eu/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/CERTAIN_WP3_D3.2_Ethical-considerations-of-AI-compliance_V1.0-delivered.pdf> ;
    dcterms:description "The GIA serves as a structured methodology for assessing how proposed policies, programmes, or initiatives may influence gender equality, either positively or negatively, and for ensuring that equality considerations are integrated into decision-making processes from the outset. In line with EIGE’s methodology, the successful implementation of a GIA consists of the following five interdependent steps:\n1. Definition of the programme’s purpose and its connection to gender equality:\nThis step involves clarifying the objectives of the proposed initiative, articulating its intended societal impact, and explicitly mapping how these aims intersect with gender-equality priorities at local, national, and EU levels. The rationale for this link must be transparent, evidence-based, and situated within broader equality frameworks and legal obligations.\n2. Assessment of gender relevance:\nHere, the programme’s potential to either affect or be affected by gender-related factors is assessed. This process includes identifying both direct and indirect effects, considering both the target user group and the wider community and potential spillover effects on gender dynamics.\n3. Conducting a gender-sensitive analysis:\nThis stage requires the collection and examination of sex-disaggregated and intersectional data, assessing existing inequalities, barriers, and enabling factors. It emphasizes understanding how different groups-women, men, and non-binary individuals-experience technology and resources differently due to structural, cultural, and socio-economic factors.\n4. Evaluating the gender impact:\nOnce the analysis is complete, the likely consequences of the programme on gender relations are evaluated. This includes both anticipated benefits and potential risks, using measurable indicators to assess inclusivity and equity outcomes.\n5. Formulating findings and proposals for improvement:\nThe final step transforms analytical insights into actionable recommendations to refine the programme to maximise gender equality benefits and minimise unintended discriminatory effects."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "SGIA / GIA - is the primary methodological basis for the five-step assessment."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "Horizon Europe ethics appraisal logic - concretely shapes risk-benefit reasoning, vulnerable-group consideration, evidence retention, and reviewability."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "OECD AI Principles; UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI; EU Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI / ALTAI - inform the human-rights, societal well-being, fairness, and stakeholder-participation dimensions."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AISystem , aidoc:PerformanceMetric , aidoc:PostMarketPerformanceEvaluationActivity , aidoc:affects , aidoc:hasIntendedPurpose , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc-lex:GenderImpactAssessment , aidoc-lex:GenderDisparityRisk , aidoc-lex:CorrectiveAction , aidoc-lex:measuredForGroup ;
