lexreq:gender-03-cq7 a lexreq:CompetencyQuestion ; rdfs:label "What proposals for improvement were formulated to maximise gender equality benefits and minimise discriminatory effects?"@en .

lexreq:gender-04 a lexreq:Requirement ;
    skos:inScheme lexreq: ;
    lexreq:framework lexreq:GenderD32 ;
    lexreq:sortKey "4-04" ;
    rdfs:label "D3.2 §5.2 - Gender-Sensitive Analysis, Indicators, and Recommendations"@en ;
    lexreq:section "D3.2 §5.2" ;
    dcterms:source "D3.2 §5.2 - Gender-Sensitive Analysis, Indicators, and Recommendations" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://certain-project.eu/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/CERTAIN_WP3_D3.2_Ethical-considerations-of-AI-compliance_V1.0-delivered.pdf> ;
    dcterms:description "In practice, this model is applied through structured surveys that gather participants’ perceptions of AI technologies across the six core dimensions (PU, PEOU, PT, SI, FC, and GI). By analysing these responses, partners can identify which factors most strongly influence acceptance-for example, whether perceived usefulness or trust plays a greater role in shaping willingness to use a specific AI system. The same survey data also enables the exploration of gendered patterns by comparing how different gender groups respond to dimensions such as ease of use, social influence, or facilitating conditions. These insights provide a practical basis for designing targeted interventions, such as improving user training, enhancing transparency, or adjusting communication strategies, with the dual aim of increasing overall acceptance and narrowing potential gender gaps.\nWithin the CERTAIN project, all research activities will embed gender-sensitive analysis as a fundamental requirement (Step 3). This entails continuously monitoring differences in technology acceptance rates, motivations for adoption, and perceived barriers among women, men, and non-binary individuals. The aim is to ensure that these differences are recognised not as incidental but as central factors shaping technology uptake and trust.\nIn parallel, CERTAIN partners will undertake a systematic evaluation of projected gender impacts during the design and development of AI systems (Step 4). This evaluation will rely on specific, measurable indicators, such as:\na) the equitable participation of women, men, and non-binary individuals in both user and stakeholder groups, and\nb) access to and control over resources that enable effective use of AI technologies.\nFinally, after each technology acceptance study, the CERTAIN project will develop tailored, evidence-based recommendations to enhance inclusivity and address identified gaps (Step 5). These recommendations will be context-specific and will be designed to ensure that the introduction and scaling of AI technologies actively contribute to narrowing, rather than widening, gender-based disparities in digital engagement and participation."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "SGIA / gender-sensitive analysis - is the primary basis for subgroup indicators, gender-related monitoring, and improvement recommendations."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "OECD AI Principles; UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI - reinforce inclusive participation, access, capability, and broader social outcomes."@en ;
    lexreq:normativeSource "EU Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI / ALTAI - are relevant where monitoring feeds fairness, accountability, and continuous-improvement controls."@en ;
    lexreq:usesTerm aidoc:AISystem , aidoc:PerformanceMetric , aidoc:PostMarketMonitoringActivity , aidoc:PostMarketPerformanceEvaluationActivity , aidoc:affects , aidoc:hasFrequency , aidoc:hasStakeholder , aidoc-lex:CorrectiveAction ;
